{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018Zt1cYBLhuWXZYW2yD5Ge5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me implement the `-p`/`--parents` flag support for `cmd/mkdir`. I need to:\n\n1. Modify `cmd/mkdir/main.go` to add `-p`/`--parents` support\n2. Add differential tests for `-p` behavior in `cmd/mkdir/mkdir_test.go`\n3. Build and test\n\nLet me think about the verbose + parents case. When `mkdir -p -v a/b/c` is called, GNU mkdir prints:\n\n\nmkdir: created directory 'a'\nmkdir: created directory 'a/b'\nmkdir: created directory 'a/b/c'\n\nBut only for directories that were actually created (not ones that already existed).\n\n`os.MkdirAll` doesn't tell us which directories it created, so for verbose mode with parents I need to walk the path components, check which exist, then create them one by one. Without verbose, `os.MkdirAll` works fine - it's only for verbose that I need custom logic.\n\nFor the implementation: when `-p` is given without verbose, I'll just use `os.MkdirAll`. When both `-p` and verbose are set, I'll iterate through each path component, check if it exists, and create it individually while printing each newly created directory.\n\nWriting the run function... For the verbose case with `-p`, I'll split the path into components and build it up incrementally, trying to create each directory one at a time. If a directory already exists, I'll skip it; if creation fails for another reason, I'll return that error. For the non-verbose case, I can just use `os.MkdirAll` for simplicity.\n\nWriting the mkdirParents function... I need to be careful about path normalization and how GNU mkdir handles the verbose output. When running `mkdir -p -v`, it prints each directory as it's created, preserving the path structure the user provided rather than normalizing it. I should probably clean the path first with `filepath.Clean` and then split it, but I need to make sure my behavior matches what `gmk