w I need to think about how the `run` function will use this — it'll loop through the directories and call `mkdirParents` when the parents flag is set, handling errors by printing them to stderr and tracking the exit code.\n\nFor the error handling when `-p` encounters an intermediate path that's a file instead of a directory, like `mkdir -p a/b/c` where `a` is a file, the function will try to create `a` with `os.Mkdir`, get an EEXIST error, check if it's actually a directory with `os.Stat`, and when that fails, return the original EEXIST error. I see the issue—gmkdir would return \"Not a directory\" instead of \"File exists\" when trying to create a path through a file. I need to check if the existing path is actually a directory, and if not, return ENOTDIR to match the expected behavior, though I'm uncertain whether the error should reference the intermediate path or the target path. Writing error handling... Simplifying the approach — if mkdir fails with EEXIST, I'll just continue to the next path component and let a subsequent mkdir fail with ENOTDIR if the existing path is actually a file rather than a directory.\n\nBut there's a catch: for the final component, if it already exists as a file, I need to error out rather than silently succeeding. So I should check whether the target path is a directory at the end, or at least handle the case where the last component hits EEXIST.\n\nLet me look at how Go's MkdirAll handles this...\n\nWriting mkdir logic... I'm simplifying this by splitting into two functions—one that delegates to the standard library when verbose mode is off, and another that handles the verbose case recursively, checking if the parent directory exists before attempting to create subdirectories.\n\nStill writing the recursive logic... I'm checking how filepath.Dir handles various path formats—it returns the parent directory, and when the parent equals the directory itself (like \"/\" or \".\"), the recursion stops. For absolute paths like \"/tmp/a\", it correctly returns \"/tmp\", and for \"/\" it returns \"/\" so the recursion terminates. Now I'm wondering about edge cases with double slashes, like what happens when filepath.Dir processes \"a//b/c\". I'm wondering whether the final directory name preserves the user's double slashes or gets cleaned like the intermediate paths—the differential tests should catch any discrepancies. For the implementation, I'm planning to keep things clean with a helper function approach: add a `parents` boolean to the options struct, wire up the `-p` and `--parents` flags in the parsing logic, then modify `run` to handle the recursive directory creation when that flag is set.\n\nHandling error messages... The issue is that when `mkdirParents` fails partway through creating nested directories, the error from `os.Mkdir` contains the actual failing path, but I'm printing the original argument instead. I need to extract the real failing path from the error and use that in the message, or return a custom error that preserves which directory actually failed.\n\nWriting error handling code... I'm going in circles here—let me refocus on what actually matters. The differential tests will catch any behavioral divergence, so I should implement the most reasonable approach and let them validate it. I'll have `mkdirParents` handle its own error formatting and verbose output to keep things consistent, then use the path from the PathError in the error message when `-p` is given, since that's likely what GNU mkdir does.\n\nLooking at how GNU mkdir actually behaves: when it fails on an intermediate directory with `-p`, it reports the error on that specific intermediate path, not the user's original argument. So extracting the path from the error is the right approach. For the `mkdirAllVerbose` case where a directory already exists but isn't actually a directory, I'm constructing a PathError with the full path, but I need to think through whether that's the right path to report.\n\nActually, I'm getting caught up in edge cases that probably aren't even being tested. Let me just implement the straightforward behavior and let the differential tests catch any issues.\n\nNow I need to modify the Go file to add support for the `-p` flag. I'm adding the `parents` field to the options struct, implementing a recursive function to create parent directories, and updating the flag parsing to recognize both `--parents` and `-p`. I'll also need to import `path/filepath` for the directory traversal logic.\n\nWriting the implementation... Writing the helper functions...\n\nNow I'm setting up the test cases for the `-p` flag, covering scenarios like creating nested directories, handling existing directories without errors, working with partially existing paths, verbose output, and multiple directory arguments. Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test assertions...\n\nNow I'm planning the edits needed for the main implementation: updating the package comment to document the new requirements, adding filepath to imports, extending the options struct with a parents flag, modifying the run function to handle parent directory creation, and adding helper functions for the recursive mkdir logic. I also need to update the flag parsing to recognize both the short `-p` and long `--parents` options.\n\nFor error handling, I should verify that the error message format stays consistent whether or not the parents flag is used. I'm also thinking through an edge case where an empty string directory could be passed to the mkdir function—filepath.Dir(\"\") returns \".\", which would then attempt to stat the current directory, and since that succeeds and is a directory, the function would proceed correctly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16951,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16951},"output_tokens":4